<BaseLayout title="SEO Fanout research examples" description="A documented owner-run OpenAI observation and a synthetic fallback demonstrate the SEO Fanout research-plan workflow with explicit evidence limits." canonicalPath="/beispiele/" pageClass="reference-page">
  <header class="reference-hero"><p class="mono-label">Example register · 4 September 2026</p><h1>Inspect a real fanout without turning it into invented SEO certainty.</h1><p>The primary example reuses one reviewed OpenAI API observation already published by AI Fanout. SEO Fanout adds a deterministic research plan; it does not rerun the provider or present the commonly operated source as independent validation.</p></header>
  <section class="case-status"><div><span class="status-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span><p class="mono-label">Current evidence state</p><h2>1 documented owner-run observation · 1 synthetic fallback</h2></div><p>The documented case preserves the original search-action scope: four exposed queries and thirteen source domains, with no invented query-to-source mapping, demand or ranking claim.</p></section>
  <article class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Documented case · OpenAI API · 27 August 2026</p><h2>Ahrefs vs Semrush for small business</h2></div><div class="case-contract"><ol><li><strong>Input</strong><span>One owner-run comparison question using the published AI Fanout protocol.</span></li><li><strong>Observed queries</strong><span>Four provider-exposed strings covering comparison, first-party pricing and small-business reviews.</span></li><li><strong>SEO output</strong><span>One comparison-guide direction and three concrete work packages instead of four query pages.</span></li><li><strong>Evidence limit</strong><span>Source domains belong to the search action, not an individual query. Search demand and page ownership remain unknown.</span></li></ol></div><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button-link" href="/tool/?example=documented">Open the documented case</a><a class="text-link" href="https://ai-fanout.com/examples/openai-observations-2026-08-27.json" rel="external">Inspect the upstream fixture <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></article>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">Synthetic fallback</p><h2>Inspect the interface without provider output.</h2></div><p>The owner-authored fallback remains available for testing the transformation when no provider observation should be used. It contains modelled ideas and zero claimed sources.</p><a class="text-link" href="/tool/?example=synthetic">Open the synthetic fallback <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section>
  <section class="reference-section"><div class="section-heading"><p class="mono-label">What the documented case proves</p><h2>A transparent transformation—not a content verdict.</h2></div><ul><li>The published fixture records input, provider, model, locale, timestamp, method and exact exposed queries.</li><li>SEO Fanout groups those queries into reproducible work packages without another AI request.</li><li>Source relationships stay at their published search-action scope.</li><li>The case does not prove consumer-product equivalence, demand, rankings, page ownership or citation probability.</li></ul></section>
